1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Point-in-time recovery. A base backup plus an unbroken chain of write-ahead log

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,900
Every committed change is first described in the write-ahead log (WAL).

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:11,600
Records accumulate in order, filling log segments one after another.

4
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:15,100
They describe low-level changes to recover the database, not a business event spreadsheet.

5
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,800
The 14:17 DELETE from the incident is in the log too, as one more committed change.

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:20,900
A base backup copies the database's storage at one point. Here, 13:00.

7
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,000
The archived WAL from that point on must be retained, segment by segment.

8
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:29,000
Choose a recovery target, such as a time just before the harmful commit.

9
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:34,600
Restore the base backup somewhere separate, then replay the log forward.

10
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,900
Replay stops at the target. The DELETE is never applied to this copy.

11
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,600
Compared with the 14:00 export, the recoverable point moves much closer to the failure.

12
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:46,900
Valid writes after 14:17 still need a decision: reconcile them, don't silently drop them.

13
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:52,500
Suppose segment 4 was never archived, or was deleted by a retention job.

14
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Replay cannot skip a gap. It cannot reach 14:16 using later segments.

15
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:57,700
A base backup that doesn't match the server version or the log breaks the chain too.

16
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:03,200
A logical dump rebuilds objects from one snapshot. The lab uses free tools.

17
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:05,900
Physical recovery reaches precise points, but only where the platform supports it.

18
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,800
Supabase Free shows no automatic backups. Check your plan before you promise an RPO.

19
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:20,400
Remember: WAL records the information needed to redo committed changes, in order. Physical recovery: restore a base backup, then replay retained WAL. Stop at a chosen target, e.g. just before a harmful commit. One missing segment or an incompatible base backup breaks the chain. It needs platform support and practice. A plan is not a tested drill.

